AB  - This study examines how volunteer service usage shapes e-government adoption in community settings. Drawing on Diffusion of Innovation (DOI) theory and the DeLone and McLean IS Success Model, it investigates how information quality, compatibility, relative advantages, and the reliability of local authority services influence citizens' intention to engage in community activities, satisfaction, and actual adoption. The results show that reliability of local authority services is the strongest predictor of participatory intention. More importantly, intention to engage in community activities—rather than satisfaction—emerges as the primary driver of adoption, indicating that system-related perceptions influence adoption mainly through a civic engagement pathway. Volunteer service usage further moderates this process in a substitutional manner, weakening the effects of compatibility and relative advantages as reliance on volunteer support increases. These findings contribute to e-government research by showing that community-level adoption is shaped less by system evaluation alone than by its translation into participatory intention within specific governance contexts. Policy implications highlight the importance of strengthening institutional reliability, fostering community participation, and positioning volunteer support as a complementary mechanism alongside improvements in system design.
