AB  - Objectives: Most research has assessed volunteerism in the individualistic socio-economic contexts of the Global North. The present study examines the motives behind volunteering in a country (Saudi Arabia) transitioning from a collectivistic socio-economic order, resting on patriarchy, to one driven by neoliberalism, striving for gender equity. In this mixed socio-economic context, unclear is the extent to which self-oriented motives, reflecting individualism, and interdependent motives, reflecting collectivism, drive voluntarism. Methods: Through purposive sampling, participants are selected from an understudied population of young females from Saudi Arabia. Women of college age are the cornerstone of the country’s socio-economic programmatic plan, and thus most likely to experience the dissonance between the old collectivistic norms and the new individualistic appeals. Results: The present study found a mixture of individualistic and collectivistic motives driving volunteering, which blurs the lines between the two types of motives. Conclusion: These findings are consistent with the phenomenon of doublethink through which career and other self-focused motives exist alongside interdependent motives without dissonance.
