AB  - Local authorities are responsible for ensuring public safety across all types of municipalities, including fire and flood protection. Volunteer Fire Brigades (OSP) play a pivotal role in this framework, engaging in fire prevention, rescue operations, and community education about hazards cooperating with the State Fire Service. This paper explores the financial mechanisms supporting OSP, especially government-earmarked funds. Despite existing literature primarily focusing on urban areas, this study identifies a significant research gap regarding financing fire protection accessibility in rural areas. By analyzing government-earmarked funds, this research aims to assess the spatial differentiation in financial assistance for OSP. The findings underscore the critical role of external funding in enhancing the operational capacity of Volunteer Fire Brigades, ultimately contributing to improved safety culture and community resilience against fire-related threats. The results of the spatial correlation analysis allowed for the identification of municipalities forming clusters related to the funds received from targeted government grants. It applied to both high-value and low-value clusters. A consequence of the obtained results was the ability to identify the most active areas in securing this type of funding.
