T1  - Assessing needs of student volunteers related to providing care to a homeless population
AU  - Forg, Rosemary E.
AU  - Paun, Olimpia
JO  - Journal of Professional Nursing
VL  - 37
IS  - 1
SP  - 73
EP  - 79
PY  - 2021
DA  - 2021/01/01/
SN  - 8755-7223
DO  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.profnurs.2020.10.008
UR  - https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S8755722320302052
KW  - Homeless healthcare
KW  - Nursing students
KW  - Service-learning
KW  - Student volunteer
AB  - Problem statement
Microsystem analysis of the free clinic run by students from Rush University at a Chicago homeless shelter revealed many unfilled volunteer shifts, and a lack of training.
Methods
As a quality improvement project to assess educational gaps and concerns, an online survey was distributed to actual and potential student volunteers.
Results
Among the 54 participants, the majority were nursing students (70.4%), had volunteered at the clinic at least once (59.3%), reported not feeling confident in their ability to care for homeless patients (64.8%), and did not believe their academic program adequately addressed care of homeless patients (70.4%). Analysis did not identify a statistically significant relationship between academic program and confidence or perceived program adequacy, likely due to the small sample size of medical and physician assistant students. Content analysis of the qualitative responses identified four categories of educational gaps and concerns—knowledge deficits, skill deficits, volunteer support, and site-specific concerns.
Conclusion
Numerous educational gaps and concerns were identified from subjective data, though no negative attitudes toward the patients were noted. These data will help design a training session for potential clinic volunteers, and suggest that further exploration of educational gaps in caring for homeless patients is warranted.
